# The dark mirror: CivOS threat model

A system that records reasons can help people challenge a decision. The same system can lend an unjustified appearance of authority to whoever controls its inputs. This document defines the prototype's trust boundaries and the failures a pilot must look for.

## Assets and actors

Protect the integrity of the decision trail, the visibility of objections, the ability to interpret corrections, and the confidentiality of material that should never enter the ledger. Keep a usable copy of the records if the local machine fails.

Relevant actors include an honest participant who makes a mistake, a contributor who deliberately misleads, a reviewer with a conflict of interest, an operator who controls the database, and an institution that uses a record to defend its own decisions. Several roles may belong to one person. The prototype does not enforce separation of duties.

The application runs locally and trusts its operator and host. Names in records are assertions, not authenticated identities. There is no distributed consensus, network identity service, permission system, or cryptographic signature workflow.

## Failure scenarios

| Threat | Concrete failure | Present boundary or response |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fabricated evidence | A convincing source description refers to a nonexistent inspection | Structural validation cannot establish source existence; reviewers must inspect evidence |
| Selective evidence | The operator records supporting material and omits a known objection | Require a human review of missing alternatives; a hash cannot reveal never-entered information |
| Authority laundering | A person calls themselves an authorized decision-maker | The application records the claim; mandate must be checked outside it |
| Identity impersonation | A record uses another participant's name | Actor labels provide attribution context, not proof of authorship |
| Database rewrite | An operator changes old records and recomputes every hash | Compare against an independently retained checkpoint; internal verification alone is insufficient |
| Truncation | Recent records disappear and the remaining prefix still verifies | Compare the current head and record count with a known external checkpoint |
| Dissent burial | An objection remains in storage but is absent from the account people read | Inspect reports and require reviewers to locate material disagreement |
| False causality | A later improvement is presented as proof the decision caused it | Separate observed outcome from causal assessment; record competing explanations |
| Source decay | A referenced document changes or disappears | Preserve permitted source versions separately and record access limits |
| Privacy exposure | A ledger or export contains identifying testimony | Use synthetic or appropriately minimized data; there is no selective erasure workflow |
| Host compromise | Malware changes the application, database, and local checkpoint | Local verification cannot establish a trusted host; use independent copies and verification where needed |
| Workload capture | Only people with time and technical fluency can participate | Measure recording burden and representation failures; offer a different process or stop |

## What the hash chain proves

Each record's digest depends on its serialized content and the previous record's digest. Verification can detect inconsistencies in the chain being checked. An independently retained earlier head lets a reviewer test whether the supplied history still matches that checkpoint.

The chain does not establish authorship, a trustworthy wall-clock time, factual truth, legal authority, or completeness. If someone replaces the entire history and there is no trusted earlier checkpoint, a self-consistent replacement can pass. A checkpoint kept beside the database is exposed to many of the same failures as the database itself.

The application treats accepted records as immutable. A machine owner can bypass the application and alter a SQLite file. Describe this as an append-only application workflow with tamper evidence under stated conditions, not unalterable storage.

## Institutional misuse

A mandatory record system could turn participation into surveillance or make dissent costly. A requirement for formal evidence could exclude people whose relevant knowledge is difficult to document. An institution could advertise procedural completeness while denying affected people any influence.

The pilot must make refusal possible, define who can challenge a record, and distinguish technical review from the authority to decide. Do not use a count of recorded objections as a proxy for freedom to object. Ask whether participants could object without retaliation, and report the limits of that assessment.

No universal truth score, personal reputation ranking, automatic sanction, or automated real-world action belongs in this prototype. Adding one would change the threat model and require a separate justification and review.

## Adversarial checks for a pilot

1. Present one well-formed false source and check whether human review catches it.
2. Correct a material observation and ask an independent reader to reconstruct both the earlier and revised rationale.
3. Record a serious objection, then check whether readers of the report notice and accurately describe it.
4. Alter or truncate a disposable database copy and compare verification with and without an external checkpoint.
5. Export and restore a synthetic ledger, then verify that the restored history matches the original checkpoint.
6. Have participants identify information that should remain outside the system and verify that none enters the export.

These exercises are proposed checks, not results. Use disposable copies and synthetic data. Stop the pilot if it exposes protected information, prevents effective challenge, or makes decision responsibility less clear. Record the failure and change the workflow before trying again.

